.TH WRITE 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
write \- send a message to another user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B write
.I user
.RI [ ttyname ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B write
copies standard input to the terminal of
.IR user .
The terminal is the first
.B USER_PROCESS
record for
.I user
in the utmp file, or the record whose line matches
.I ttyname
when that is given.
.I ttyname
is the line name relative to
.IR /dev ,
for example
.BR tty2 ;
it may not contain
.B /
or begin with
a dot.
.PP
The terminal
.I /dev/line
is opened non-blocking and without following symbolic links, and must
be a terminal.
.B write
then sends a banner
.PP
.EX
Message from \fIsender\fP on \fIline\fP ...
.EE
.PP
preceded by a bell, where
.I sender
comes from
.BR getlogin (3)
or the password entry of the real user ID, and
.I line
is the name of the sender's standard input terminal
.RB ( ?
if it is not a terminal).
Standard input is then relayed line by line until end of file, followed
by
.BR EOF .
Newlines and tabs pass through, carriage returns are dropped, other
control characters are shown as
.BI ^ X
and bytes with the high bit set as
.BI M\- X\c
\&.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The terminal was opened and the message sent.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong number of arguments, invalid
.IR ttyname ,
the utmp file could not be read,
.I user
is not logged in (on that terminal), or the terminal could not be
opened or is not a terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/run/utmp
Current login records.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
$ echo "lunch?" | write alice tty2
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tty (1),
.BR wall (1),
.BR who (1),
.BR utmp (5)
.SH BUGS
There is no interactive two-way mode and no
.B mesg
permission check; delivery depends solely on whether the sender can
open the recipient's terminal for writing.
Errors writing to the terminal after it has been opened are ignored.
